"""Independent variable-force teaching, beginning with book sections 17.1–17.2."""
import sympy as s
lessons=[];models=[]
def T(v):return s.latex(s.sympify(v))
def D(*rows):return r'\[\begin{gathered}'+r'\\'.join(rows)+r'\end{gathered}\]'
def E(code,title,question,solution,kind,**data):
 uid='MVAR-E-'+code;models.append(dict(id=uid,kind=kind,**data));return dict(title=title,question=question,solution=solution,verification=uid)
def L(key,title,summary,objective,before,ideas,method,mistakes,examples):
 lessons.append(dict(key=key,id='fm_variable_'+key,title=title,summary=summary,objective=objective,before=before,ideas=ideas,method='<ol>'+''.join('<li>'+m+'</li>' for m in method)+'</ol>',mistakes=mistakes,examples=examples))
base='/courses/maths4u-9231-mechanics/topics/fm_variable_'
L('time','Time-dependent forces and complete trajectories','Integrate with the initial conditions, then distinguish displacement, distance and a change of model.',
 'Construct signed velocity and displacement functions and apply them only over their physical time intervals.',
 '<p>Review differentiation, definite integration, exponentials and logarithms. The <a href="/courses/maths4u-9231-mechanics/topics/fm_projectiles_components">signed-components lesson</a> explains direction conventions. A force law supplies acceleration only after division by the moving mass.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Three linked quantities, one positive direction</h3>'''+D(r'v=\frac{dx}{dt}',r'a=\frac{dv}{dt}=\frac{d^2x}{dt^2}',r'F_{\rm resultant}=ma')+r'''<p>Displacement and velocity are signed; speed is |v|. A negative acceleration means acceleration in the negative coordinate direction. It reduces speed only while velocity is positive. State the mass and all real force directions before forming the differential equation.</p><h3>Integrate twice, using two initial conditions</h3><p>When acceleration is a known function of time, integrate to get v(t), apply the velocity condition, integrate again for x(t), and apply the position condition. If the given relation couples v and x, use v=dx/dt and separate variables when possible.</p>'''+D(r'v(t)=u+\int_0^t a(s)\,ds',r'x(t)=x_0+\int_0^t v(s)\,ds')+r'''<p>A constant of integration is not automatically zero because t=0 was chosen. For x′=f(t)x, division by x needs attention to the possible rest-at-zero solution; the nonzero initial state determines the branch used.</p><h3>Time-dependent factors can make separation possible</h3><p>An equation v′=f(t)h(v) separates into dv/h(v)=f(t)dt wherever the divisor is nonzero. Check any constant-velocity solution lost by division and then apply the initial condition. Differentiate the proposed answer and check the initial value before using it.</p><h3>Distance counts both directions</h3>'''+D(r'\Delta x=\int_{t_0}^{t_1}v(t)\,dt',r's=\int_{t_0}^{t_1}|v(t)|\,dt')+r'''<p>Locate every velocity zero in the required interval and split the distance integral at each actual reversal. A turning point has v=0; a return to the origin has x=0. They are different events. A force changing sign is not itself proof that velocity changes sign at that time.</p><h3>A switch starts a new stage</h3><p>If a finite force is switched off with no impulse, position and velocity remain continuous. Use their values at the switch as the next stage's initial conditions. Do not continue the old acceleration law after its stated cutoff. The resultant work equals the change in kinetic energy even when the force varies.</p><h3>Limits are not events by themselves</h3><p>A velocity can stay positive for every finite time while tending to zero. Inspect the displacement function or its integral to decide whether the total distance is finite. A formula with a finite-time pole has a shorter domain than all t≥0, even if it can be evaluated algebraically on another branch.</p>''',
 ['Choose a positive direction and form the signed resultant-force equation.','Integrate or separate the appropriate differential equation.','Use every initial condition and verify the answer by differentiation.','Find turns, returns and switches in the correct order.','Use absolute velocity for distance and state the interval where the model applies.'],
 ['Divide resultant force by mass before integrating.','Initial position and initial velocity supply separate constants.','Negative acceleration does not always mean decreasing speed.','A turning time does not equal a return time.','A force switched off does not reset velocity to zero.'],[
 E('T1','1 · Both integration constants matter',r'<p>A 3 kg particle has resultant force \(F=6-3t\) N. Initially x=1 m and v=3 m s⁻¹. Find x(t), v(t), and the first non-negative turning time for this model.</p>',D(r'a=2-t',r'v=3+2t-\frac12t^2',r'x=1+3t+t^2-\frac16t^3',r'v=0\quad\Longrightarrow\quad t=2+\sqrt{10}')+'<p>The other root is negative. The force is zero at t=2, when velocity is still positive; it is not the turning instant.</p>','time_poly',mass=3,force='6-3*t',x0=1,u=3,v='3+2*t-t**2/2',x='1+3*t+t**2-t**3/6',turn='2+sqrt(10)'),
 E('T2','2 · A displacement–velocity relation is an ODE',r'<p>A 2 kg particle satisfies \(v/x=3/(t+2)\) for t≥0 and starts at x=8 m. Find its trajectory and resultant force.</p>',D(r'\frac1x\frac{dx}{dt}=\frac3{t+2}',r'\ln x=3\ln(t+2)+C',r'x=(t+2)^3',r'v=3(t+2)^2',r'F=2\frac{dv}{dt}=12(t+2)')+'<p>The initial displacement fixes the multiplicative constant at one. The trajectory stays positive, so the division by x is valid on this branch.</p>','x_ratio',mass=2,rate='3/(t+2)',x0=8,x='(t+2)**3',v='3*(t+2)**2',force='12*(t+2)'),
 E('T3','3 · Positive velocity with finite total travel',r'<p>A particle starts at O with velocity 9 m s⁻¹ and has acceleration \(-18/(1+t)^3\) m s⁻² for t≥0. Find its displacement and describe its eventual position.</p>',D(r'v=\frac9{(1+t)^2}',r'x=9\left(1-\frac1{1+t}\right)',r'0\le x\lt9,\qquad v\gt0')+'<p>The particle approaches x=9 m without reaching it at finite time. Its total distance is 9 m because velocity stays positive and the displacement has that finite limit.</p>','time_decay',p=18,u=9,v='9/(1+t)**2',x='9*(1-1/(1+t))',limit=9),
 E('T4','4 · A reversal before a force switches off',r'<p>A 2 kg particle starts at O with velocity 4 m s⁻¹. Its resultant force is \(-4t\) N until t=3 s, and zero thereafter. Find distance travelled by t=3, the first return time to O and work done in the first three seconds.</p>',D(r'v=4-t^2,\qquad x=4t-\frac13t^3',r't_{\rm turn}=2,\quad x(2)=\frac{16}3',r'x(3)=3,\qquad v(3)=-5',r's=2x(2)-x(3)=\frac{23}3',r'T=3+\frac35=\frac{18}5',r'W=\frac12(2)(25-16)=9')+'<p>After t=3 the particle moves uniformly leftwards. Extending the earlier cubic trajectory into that stage would give the wrong return time.</p>','time_switch',mass=2,force='-4*t',u=4,cutoff=3,v='4-t**2',x='4*t-t**3/3',turn=2,distance='23/3',returnTime='18/5',work=9)])
L('position','Position-dependent force and the physical velocity branch','Use the chain rule to find v², then select the direction and test whether a candidate point is reachable.',
 'Link force, velocity and position, including finite-time and asymptotic boundaries.',
 '<p>Complete <a href="'+base+'time">signed trajectories and initial conditions</a>. Review separable equations, the chain rule and improper integrals. Use a monotone segment when treating velocity as a function of position.</p>',
 r'''<h3>The chain rule changes the independent variable</h3>'''+D(r'\frac{dv}{dt}=\frac{dv}{dx}\frac{dx}{dt}=v\frac{dv}{dx}',r'\frac12(v^2-u^2)=\int_{x_0}^{x}\frac{F(s)}m\,ds')+r'''<p>The integral is also the work–energy relation divided by mass. Do not integrate F/m directly to obtain v when x is the integration variable: the left side is ∫v dv. A constant-force SUVAT formula is inappropriate for a general variable force.</p><h3>A squared velocity has lost its direction</h3><p>After obtaining v², choose the sign using the initial velocity and the current segment of motion. Use the square root only where v²≥0. A zero can describe a genuine turning point, an asymptotically approached point, or the end of the stated force law. Test rather than guessing.</p><h3>Time comes from one more separation</h3>'''+D(r't-t_0=\int_{x_0}^{x}\frac{ds}{v(s)}')+r'''<p>If the integral diverges as x approaches a candidate endpoint, that point is not reached at finite time. A simple zero of v with v proportional to the remaining distance produces a logarithmic divergence. Conversely, a formula growing without bound at finite time must be restricted before that singular time.</p><h3>An inverse-square attraction has an escape threshold</h3><p>For acceleration −k/x², launched outwards from x=a with speed u,</p>'''+D(r'v^2=u^2+2k\left(\frac1x-\frac1a\right)')+r'''<p>A finite upper turning position exists when u²&lt;2k/a. At equality the particle can travel arbitrarily far while its speed tends to zero; above equality it has a positive limiting outward speed. The initial direction and the x&gt;0 domain remain part of this statement.</p><h3>Velocity-dependent laws can also simplify in position</h3><p>If acceleration has a factor v, the equation v dv/dx may cancel that factor on a nonzero-velocity branch. Check the lost v=0 case separately. For a constant-power drive opposed by quadratic resistance, working with v³ as a function of x is often simpler than integrating with respect to time.</p><h3>Verify the proposed function</h3><p>Check v dv/dx against the original signed acceleration, the initial speed and position, and the allowed coordinate interval. If a time formula is available, also check dx/dt=v(x(t)). These checks catch a missing factor of two and an incorrect square-root sign.</p>''',
 ['Choose the signed coordinate and one physically consistent branch.','Replace acceleration by v dv/dx and separate or integrate.','Apply the initial velocity condition to v², then select the sign of v.','Integrate dx/v if time or reachability is required.','Check every domain boundary and differentiate back into the force law.'],
 ['The integral of acceleration with respect to x gives v²/2, not v.','A square root does not choose the velocity direction for you.','A zero of v² need not be reached in finite time.','Do not divide by v without considering any lost rest solution.','Continue a branch only up to its first valid boundary.'],[
 E('P1','1 · Integrate v dv, not just dv',r'<p>A particle starts at x=0 with speed 8 m s⁻¹. For x≥0 its acceleration is \(-12x^2\) m s⁻². Find its forward velocity and first turning position.</p>',D(r'v\frac{dv}{dx}=-12x^2',r'\frac12(v^2-64)=-4x^3',r'v=\sqrt{64-8x^3}',r'0\le x\le2,\qquad x_{\rm turn}=2')+'<p>The positive square root describes the outward segment. At the turn acceleration is −48 m s⁻², so a negative-velocity return segment follows if the force law continues to hold.</p>','position_poly',coefficient=12,u2=64,v2='64-8*x**3',turn=2),
 E('P2','2 · Find a finite upper turning position',r'<p>A particle moves outwards from x=2 m with speed 4 m s⁻¹ under acceleration \(-20/x^2\) m s⁻². Find v² and the greatest distance from the origin.</p>',D(r'\frac12(v^2-16)=\int_2^x-\frac{20}{s^2}\,ds',r'v^2=\frac{40}{x}-4',r'x_{\max}=10')+'<p>The outward branch has v>0 for 2≤x<10. This is below the escape threshold because the initial speed squared 16 is below 2k/a=20.</p>','inverse_square',k=20,initialX=2,u2=16,v2='40/x-4',turn=10),
 E('P3','3 · A zero velocity reached only in a limit',r'<p>A particle initially at x=1 m has forward velocity \(v=4(3-x)\) m s⁻¹ on x<3. Find its acceleration as a function of x and its trajectory. Can it reach x=3 at finite time?</p>',D(r'a=v\frac{dv}{dx}=16(x-3)',r'\frac{dx}{3-x}=4\,dt',r'x=3-2e^{-4t}',r'x\lt3\quad\text{for every finite }t\ge0')+'<p>Velocity stays positive and tends to zero. The time integral diverges logarithmically as x approaches 3; the zero in the velocity formula is not a finite-time reversal.</p>','position_limit',b=3,c=4,x0=1,v='4*(3-x)',acceleration='16*(x-3)',trajectory='3-2*exp(-4*t)'),
 E('P4','4 · An outward model with a finite-time pole',r'<p>A particle starts at x=0 with velocity 4 m s⁻¹ and has acceleration \(2x^3+8x\) m s⁻² on x≥0. Find x(t) and the maximal non-negative finite branch beginning at t=0.</p>',D(r'v^2=16+x^4+8x^2=(x^2+4)^2',r'v=x^2+4',r'\int_0^x\frac{ds}{s^2+4}=t',r'\frac12\tan^{-1}\left(\frac x2\right)=t',r'x=2\tan(2t)',r'0\le t\lt\frac\pi4')+'<p>The coordinate grows without bound at π/4 in this ideal model. The tangent formula beyond its first pole is not a continuation of the specified forward trajectory.</p>','position_blowup',acceleration='2*x**3+8*x',u2=16,v='x**2+4',trajectory='2*tan(2*t)',bound='pi/4')])
L('resistance','Resistance, first stops and changes of direction','Separate the velocity equation and keep drag opposite to the actual motion.',
 'Solve resistance models on valid ascent, descent and stopping branches without inventing a restart.',
 '<p>Complete <a href="'+base+'time">time-dependent motion</a> and <a href="'+base+'position">the v dv/dx link</a>. Review logarithmic integration and the substitution v=b tanθ for a quadratic denominator. All forces must be resolved along the chosen positive axis.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Speed and signed velocity play different roles</h3><p>A resistance opposes the instantaneous motion. If signed velocity is w, linear drag has signed force −cw, and quadratic drag has signed force −kw|w|. A specified force that always points in one fixed direction is a different model. Its sign need not reverse with the motion.</p><h3>Separate a velocity-only equation</h3>'''+D(r'\frac{dv}{dt}=f(v)',r't-t_0=\int_{v_0}^{v}\frac{ds}{f(s)}')+r'''<p>Keep the constant-velocity solutions f(v)=0 that division would remove. With positive forward speed and deceleration −(A+Bv), integrate 1/(A+Bv). Stop using the forward formula at its first zero unless the next force model has been justified.</p><h3>A square can hide a branch restriction</h3><p>For resistance proportional to √v, separation first gives an expression for the non-negative quantity √v. That expression cannot be continued after it becomes negative and then squared to produce a supposed restart. If no drive acts and the force is zero at rest, the later solution remains v=0.</p><h3>Ascent and descent require separate force equations</h3><p>For upward speed v during ascent with quadratic resistance, gravity and resistance both act downwards. Using a downward speed s during descent reverses the direction of resistance relative to the coordinate:</p>'''+D(r'\frac{dv}{dt}=-g-kv^2\quad\text{(ascent)}',r'\frac{ds}{dt}=g-ks^2\quad\text{(descent)}')+r'''<p>Use the top state as the descent initial condition. The return speed is smaller than launch speed because resistance does negative work on both legs. Conservation of mechanical energy over the whole journey is therefore invalid.</p><h3>A quadratic denominator stays within ordinary calculus</h3>'''+D(r'\int\frac{dv}{b^2+v^2}=\frac1b\tan^{-1}\left(\frac vb\right)+C')+r'''<p>This follows from v=b tanθ. For b²−v² use partial fractions and logarithms on the relevant branch. Inverse hyperbolic functions and an integrating factor are not required here.</p><h3>Test the first event</h3><p>Choose the first positive stopping or turning time, check that the stated position can be reached before it, and use speed |w| rather than a signed component when giving a speed. For friction, its law at permanent rest may differ from its sliding value.</p>''',
 ['Choose a positive direction and write the signed weight, drag and other forces.','Separate the equation and apply the initial speed.','Keep root/logarithm sign restrictions and any lost constant solution.','Find the first zero or switch and form the next branch separately.','Check force direction, event order and the actual distance or speed requested.'],
 ['A resistance always opposes motion; a fixed-direction force need not.','A squared formula does not justify a negative square-root branch.','Do not extend a forward-speed formula through a first stop automatically.','Gravity and resistance have different relative signs on ascent and descent.','Do not conserve mechanical energy through a resistive round trip.'],[
 E('V1','1 · Stop at the first zero of the forward solution',r'<p>A trolley starts at O with speed 10 m s⁻¹ and obeys \(dv/dt=-(v+2)/3\) while moving forwards. There is no drive and it remains at rest after its first stop. Find the stopping time and distance.</p>',D(r'\frac{dv}{v+2}=-\frac13\,dt',r'v=12e^{-t/3}-2',r'T=3\ln6',r'x=36(1-e^{-t/3})-2t',r's=30-6\ln6')+'<p>The formula is used only for 0≤t≤T. A later negative algebraic value would not describe a new forward speed under the stated post-stop model.</p>','linear_stop',A=2,B='1/3',u=10,v='12*exp(-t/3)-2',trajectory='36*(1-exp(-t/3))-2*t',stop='3*log(6)',distance='30-6*log(6)'),
 E('V2','2 · The unsquared expression must stay non-negative',r'<p>A particle starts at speed 9 m s⁻¹ with \(dv/dt=-3\sqrt v\) while moving. At rest the force is zero and there is no drive. Find its first stopping time, distance before stopping and later speed.</p>',D(r'2\sqrt v=6-3t',r'v=\left(3-\frac32t\right)^2\quad(0\le t\le2)',r'T=2,\qquad s=\int_0^2v\,dt=6',r'v=0\quad(t\ge2)')+'<p>After t=2, the expression 3−3t/2 is negative. Squaring it would invent a restart and would no longer satisfy the original resistance equation.</p>','sqrt_stop',k=3,u=9,v='(3-3*t/2)**2',stop=2,distance=6),
 E('V3','3 · Upward motion under linear air resistance',r'<p>A particle is projected vertically upwards at 25 m s⁻¹. During ascent it obeys \(dv/dt=-10-v/5\), with upward positive and x=0 at launch. Find its trajectory and greatest height.</p>',D(r'v=75e^{-t/5}-50',r'T=5\ln\left(\frac32\right)',r'x=375(1-e^{-t/5})-50t',r'H=125-250\ln\left(\frac32\right)')+'<p>These ascent expressions end at v=0. For descent the signed-velocity convention must still keep resistance opposite to the motion.</p>','up_linear',g=10,c='1/5',u=25,v='75*exp(-t/5)-50',trajectory='375*(1-exp(-t/5))-50*t',stop='5*log(3/2)',height='125-250*log(3/2)'),
 E('V4','4 · Derive a separate descent law',r'<p>A particle is launched upwards at \(\sqrt{3g/k}\). Weight and quadratic resistance \(mk s^2\), opposing motion at speed s, act throughout. Find the maximum height and return speed to launch level.</p>',D(r'v\frac{dv}{dx}=-g-kv^2',r'g+kv^2=4g\,e^{-2kx}',r'H=\frac{\ln4}{2k}=\frac{\ln2}{k}',r's\frac{ds}{dy}=g-ks^2',r's^2=\frac gk(1-e^{-2ky})',r's_{\rm return}=\sqrt{\frac{3g}{4k}}')+'<p>Here y is distance downwards from the top. At return y=H. The return speed is half the launch speed, consistently with energy dissipated on both legs.</p>','quadratic_roundtrip',initial2='3*g/k',height='log(2)/k',return2='3*g/(4*k)')])
L('limits','Limiting speeds, finite travel and constant power','Use the force balance as a candidate limit, then prove reachability from the trajectory.',
 'Distinguish asymptotic limits from finite events and solve constant-power motion using the appropriate variable.',
 '<p>Complete <a href="'+base+'resistance">resistance branches</a> and <a href="'+base+'position">position–velocity links</a>. Review exponential limits, logarithmic integrals and power as force times velocity. The constant-power driving law used below is specified for positive speed.</p>',
 r'''<h3>Zero acceleration identifies a candidate limiting speed</h3><p>For v′=A−cv with A,c positive, the equilibrium speed is L=A/c. Separation gives</p>'''+D(r'v=L+(u-L)e^{-ct}',r'x=x_0+Lt+\frac{u-L}{c}(1-e^{-ct})')+r'''<p>If u&lt;L the speed increases; if u&gt;L it decreases. Unless u=L initially, the exponential difference is nonzero at every finite time. The balance A−cL=0 alone does not prove that the trajectory tends to L or reaches it.</p><h3>Quadratic drag has two non-equilibrium branches</h3><p>For v′=k(L²−v²), partial fractions give a logarithm involving (v−L)/(v+L). Below L and above L have different initial signs; keep the correct branch and exclude v=L only after recording it as a constant solution. An exponential form avoids needing inverse hyperbolic notation.</p><h3>Zero limiting speed does not decide total distance</h3><p>A positive speed proportional to (1+t)⁻² has a convergent displacement integral. A speed proportional to (1+t)⁻¹ or (1+t)⁻¹/² has an unbounded integral. Check ∫v dt or x(t), not merely the limit of v. Also distinguish a finite limiting position from a finite-time stopping position.</p><h3>Constant power is not constant force</h3>'''+D(r'\mathcal P=F_{\rm drive}v',r'F_{\rm drive}=\frac{\mathcal P}{v}')+r'''<p>For a particle of mass m, write the driving power as mP and quadratic resistance as mkv². On v&gt;0,</p>'''+D(r'v\frac{dv}{dx}=\frac Pv-kv^2',r'w=v^3,\qquad\frac{dw}{dx}=3(P-kw)',r'w=\frac Pk+\left(u^3-\frac Pk\right)e^{-3k(x-x_0)}')+r'''<p>This follows by separation in w. The limiting speed is the cube root of P/k, and the actual driving power is mP. Do not set P equal to a force. After a switch, x₀ and u refer to the switch state, not the original launch.</p><h3>A requested target may not be reachable</h3><p>If a target speed lies strictly below an approached upper limit, the logarithmic event expression can give a finite time or distance. Equality is a limiting event; a larger target is not reached on that branch. Check positivity of every logarithm argument and the direction of change before accepting a formula.</p>''',
 ['Find constant-speed candidates from the signed force balance.','Solve with the actual initial speed and select the correct branch.','Check limits, monotonicity and finite-time denominators.','For a constant-power drive consider w=v³ as a function of position.','Test strict target reachability and distinguish time, distance and limiting position.'],
 ['A zero acceleration equation is not itself proof of finite-time attainment.','An initial speed above a terminal value must decrease towards it.','A speed tending to zero can still produce unbounded total distance.','Constant driving power gives a force inversely proportional to speed.','At a switch, use the switch position and velocity as the new initial state.'],[
 E('L1','1 · Reach a lower target before the limit',r'<p>A particle starts at x=0 with speed 2 m s⁻¹ and obeys \(dv/dt=18-3v\). Find the time and displacement at speed 5 m s⁻¹, and state its limiting speed.</p>',D(r'v=6-4e^{-3t}',r'x=6t-\frac43(1-e^{-3t})',r'v=5\quad\Longrightarrow\quad T=\frac{\ln4}{3}',r'x(T)=2\ln4-1',r'v\longrightarrow6')+'<p>The target 5 is attained at finite time. The limit 6 is not, because the exponential term never vanishes at finite time.</p>','linear_limit',A=18,c=3,u=2,v='6-4*exp(-3*t)',trajectory='6*t-4*(1-exp(-3*t))/3',target=5,time='log(4)/3',distance='2*log(4)-1',limit=6),
 E('L2','2 · A quadratic-drag model from rest',r'<p>A particle starts from rest at x=0 with \(dv/dt=16-v^2\). Find v(t), then the time and distance needed to reach half its limiting speed. Use exponentials.</p>',D(r'v=4\frac{e^{8t}-1}{e^{8t}+1}',r'v=2\quad\Longrightarrow\quad T=\frac{\ln3}{8}',r'v\frac{dv}{dx}=16-v^2',r'v^2=16(1-e^{-2x})',r'x=\frac12\ln\left(\frac43\right)')+'<p>The limiting speed is 4 m s⁻¹. The selected branch rises from zero and stays below 4 at every finite time.</p>','quadratic_limit',A=16,k=1,u=0,v='4*(exp(8*t)-1)/(exp(8*t)+1)',target=2,time='log(3)/8',distance='log(4/3)/2',limit=4),
 E('L3','3 · Slowing forever without finite total travel',r'<p>A particle starts at O with speed 3 m s⁻¹ and has \(dv/dt=-v^3/6\). Find its speed and displacement, and decide whether total distance is finite.</p>',D(r'\frac1{v^2}=\frac19+\frac t3',r'v=\frac3{\sqrt{1+3t}}',r'x=2(\sqrt{1+3t}-1)')+'<p>The speed is positive for every finite t and tends to zero, but x grows without bound. This contrasts with the finite-distance example in the time-dependent lesson.</p>','cubic_drag',coefficient='1/6',u=3,v='3/sqrt(1+3*t)',trajectory='2*(sqrt(1+3*t)-1)'),
 E('L4','4 · Change variable to the cube of speed',r'<p>A 1 kg particle moves forwards under a constant-power drive of 16 W and resistance \(v^2/4\) N. Initially x=0 and v=2 m s⁻¹. Find v³ as a function of x, the limiting speed and the distance to reach v=3.</p>',D(r'v^2\frac{dv}{dx}=16-\frac14v^3',r'w=v^3,\qquad\frac{dw}{dx}=48-\frac34w',r'v^3=64-56e^{-3x/4}',r'v_{\rm limit}=4',r'x=\frac43\ln\left(\frac{56}{37}\right)')+'<p>The target 3 lies between the initial speed and the limit, so it is reached at finite positive distance. The driving force is 16/v N, not 16 N.</p>','constant_power',P=16,k='1/4',u=2,cube='64-56*exp(-3*x/4)',limit=4,target=3,distance='4*log(56/37)/3')])
